<task id="184" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>Two changes in UAC's binder and invite code: E11 email-binds a contact only on this environment's own intent (an active grant or a pending invite), and inviting someone who already has a CIAM account succeeds</title>
    <phase>Session 28 — UAC (owner round 96, 2026-10-12)</phase>
    <status>pending</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Identity binding and tenant isolation between customers (task 141 surface), plus the invite endpoint.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Auth and tenant-isolation code. A wrong bind merges two people or gives one customer's contact to another's sign-in.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Its own worktree from master. Touches ContactBindingDecision, ContactIdentityBinder and InviteExternalUserEndpoint, which no open UAC PR touches.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>none</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security, tenant-isolation, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>1 day</estimated-effort>
    <origin>
      customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1 coordination note `notes/coordination/2026-10-12-to-uac-r2-t240d-ciam-contacts.md` (items 1 and 2), on origin/work/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1.
      Owner decision, round 96, 2026-10-12: "the no-schema rule". E11 binds only when this environment already has an active external grant for the contact, or a pending invite of it.
      Provisioning's condition: Y's own grant or Y's own pending invite must exist before E11 binds.
      Spike S1 measured that a second `POST /users` with the same email returns 400 Request_BadRequest / ObjectConflict on proxyAddresses.
    </origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Implement both changes on a branch from master and open a PR into master. Read task 141's binding model first: the header comments in ContactBindingDecision.cs and ContactIdentityBinder.cs, and notes/handoffs/INCOMING-141-workforce-tenant-list.md. Do not change CiamUserProvisioningService.cs beyond what (2) strictly needs; provisioning's T240d owns its other changes.</prompt>

  <goal>(1) E11 INTENT GATE. Today `ContactBindingDecision` E11 (~:659 on master) returns `BindByEmail` for an unbound contact whose email matches. The CIAM plane can reach it with a token for any customer's audience, so a CIAM user that customer Y never invited is bound to Y's contact.

The new rule: E11 binds only when THIS environment shows intent for that contact. Intent means:
- (a) the contact has at least one ACTIVE `sprk_externalrecordaccess` grant (not expired, not deactivated); or
- (b) the contact has a PENDING invite, recorded by the existing invite flow (`InviteExternalUserEndpoint.ProvisionAsync` / `/invite-and-grant`), and not yet bound.

Requirements:
- Find what the current invite flow writes that identifies "invited, not yet signed in". Use ONLY existing columns or records; this is the owner's no-schema rule.
- If nothing existing distinguishes an invited contact from one that was merely typed in, STOP and escalate. Do not add a column.
- When there is no intent, deny with a new specific deny code (for example `contact_not_invited`). There is no collision flag, because no other person is involved.
- The read must fail CLOSED: a fault means deny, or a 503 per the binder's existing fault mapping. It must never bind.
- Apply the rule on the CIAM plane. On the workforce plane, keep today's behaviour and record why in the PR. The workforce plane is under separate owner discussion (Model 1 workforce sign-in).
- The data the decision needs is read through the binder's existing lookups. Add the grant or invite fact to the decision's input, following the existing `ContactLookup` / `references` pattern. Keep the decision pure.

(2) EXISTING CIAM ACCOUNT. When `CiamUserProvisioningService.CreateCiamUserAsync` fails with a Graph 400 whose error is `ObjectConflict` on proxyAddresses:
- Treat it as "the person already has a CIAM account". Do not fail the invite.
- Keep the contact pending, so it carries the invite evidence that (1) relies on.
- Send a "sign in with your existing account" email instead of the onboarding email. Reuse the existing email service. If a new template method is needed, add it in the same pattern.
- The invite's outcome names this case (for example "ExistingAccount").
- Do NOT look up or bind the existing CIAM user's oid at invite time. Binding happens on first sign-in under rule (1).
- Any other Graph error keeps today's failure path.

(3) PR BODY. Give the CLAUDE.md §11 answers for any new deny code, outcome or email method. List the provisioning coordination note as the source, and say that provisioning reviews the cross-customer CIAM case.</goal>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="owner round 96">No new Dataverse column or table. If one is needed, escalate.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-028 / §9 / §6">No new credential, identity or permission. This is auth code, so the owner signs off at review.</constraint>
    <constraint source="task 141">E10's collision rules, the oid-first resolution, and "never clear or re-point a link" stay exactly as they are.</constraint>
    <constraint source="git">Stage explicit paths only. Never force-push, never bare `git stash`, never `--no-verify`. Do not merge.</constraint>
    <constraint source="machine">Shared, loaded machine. Kill only your own PIDs.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <files>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ContactBindingDecision.cs</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ContactIdentityBinder.cs</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/InviteExternalUserEndpoint.cs</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Registration/CiamUserProvisioningService.cs</file>
